.TH VAINFO "1" "September 2026" "vainfo" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
vainfo \- display information from the VA-API driver
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B vainfo
[\fI\-\-display\fR \fIdrm\fR|\fIx11\fR]
[\fI\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fI\-a\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
This program displays information about the VA-API (video acceleration)
driver installed in the system: the library version, the driver it loaded
and every profile and entrypoint (decode, encode, video processing) the
driver offers for the graphics device.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-display\fR \fIdrm\fR|\fIx11\fR
The back end to open the device through.  \fIdrm\fR opens the render node
directly and needs no X server; \fIx11\fR asks the running X server for
the device.  Without the option the X11 back end is tried when DISPLAY is
set, the DRM one otherwise.
.TP
\fB\-\-device\fR \fIpath\fR
The render node for the DRM back end, /dev/dri/renderD128 by default.
.TP
\fB\-a\fR, \fB\-\-all\fR
Print every attribute of every configuration as well.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME
The driver to load in place of the one chosen from the graphics device's
kernel driver (for Intel graphics: iHD, then i965).
.TP
.B LIBVA_DRIVERS_PATH
Where the drivers are looked for, /usr/lib/dri by default.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ffmpeg (1),
.BR gst-inspect-1.0 (1),
.BR drminfo (1)
